% -------------------- 1. PROJECT DESCRIPTION --------------------
\chapter{Project Description}

A full-stack railway service management system that provides integrated solutions for train passengers including complaint management, food ordering, emergency services, feedback submission, and lost \& found tracking. Features role-based dashboards for users, staff, and administrators with secure JWT authentication.

Railway-Madad is implemented with a Node.js and Express backend, MongoDB persistence through Mongoose, and train-aware operational filtering in all major workflows. The platform exposes RESTful APIs for passenger-facing assistance and operational governance. Core backend modules include complaint lifecycle handling, staff command flows, catering order state transitions, emergency response coordination, and lost-and-found management with optional media upload support.

The platform supports four practical role layers: \textbf{User}, \textbf{Staff}, \textbf{Admin}, and \textbf{Superadmin}, each with role-scoped workflows and authorization boundaries. Users can register complaints with evidence, track complaint history, place catering orders, report emergencies, submit feedback, and manage lost/found records. Staff can authenticate against train context, resolve domain-mapped complaints, and process command notifications from administrators. Admins manage train-level staff rosters, monitor complaints and orders, dispatch operational commands, and update incident states. Superadmins operate system-wide analytics and governance endpoints for cross-train operational intelligence. This layered model ensures strong access control, clear ownership of actions, and auditable operational flow.

The application architecture emphasizes modular separation of concerns. Each module has dedicated route files, controller logic, and model schemas. Cross-cutting concerns such as logging, file upload, cloud media storage, and OpenAPI documentation are integrated in reusable infrastructure layers. Logging is implemented through request/error middleware, media uploads are streamed to Cloudinary, and API documentation is served through Swagger UI. The backend also includes seed logic for bootstrap administration and a train registry for operational scoping.

A significant enhancement in Version 2.0 is the comprehensive test-driven quality layer built using Jest and Supertest. The testing strategy combines route-contract tests (covering all registered endpoints and authorization paths) with controller-level unit tests for business rules and edge cases. Tests run in isolation without real server startup, database dependency, or third-party network calls, making the suite suitable for fast local verification and scalable CI/CD execution.

