\begin{longtable}{>{\bfseries}p{0.32\textwidth} p{0.14\textwidth} p{0.5\textwidth}}
\toprule
\rowcolor{TableHeader}
\textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Relationship} & \textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Cardinality} & \textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Constraint / Note} \\
\midrule
\endfirsthead
\toprule
\rowcolor{TableHeader}
\textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Relationship} & \textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Cardinality} & \textcolor{TableHeaderText}{Constraint / Note} \\
\midrule
\endhead
User to Complaint & 1:N & One user can submit many complaints; each complaint belongs to one userId. \\
User to Catering Order & 1:N & One user can place many catering orders; each order references a single user. \\
User to LostFound Item & 1:N & One user can create multiple lost/found records; ownership is enforced for delete/resolve. \\
User to Emergency & 1:N & One user may create multiple emergency requests over time. \\
Admin to Command & 1:N & An admin can dispatch multiple commands/notices. \\
Staff to Command & 1:N & A staff member receives multiple commands and can mark each as read. \\
Train to Complaint & 1:N & Complaints are train-scoped through trainNumber field. \\
Train to Catering Order & 1:N & Catering records are train-scoped for operational filtering. \\
Train to Emergency & 1:N & Emergency incidents are associated with a train route context. \\
Food to Catering Item & 1:N (embedded usage) & Catering order line items reference foodItem IDs and preserve priceAtOrder. \\
Staff Role to Complaint Domain & N:1 mapping constraint & Role-specific staff resolution is constrained by complaint issueDomain matching staff role. \\
\bottomrule
\end{longtable}

